Hello. I'm attempting to write some KubeJS scripting to manually override part of AppleSkin's tooltip rendering. 

This is part of a broader plan to have give foods dynamic food data and have it be rendered by AppleSkin. Dynamic data is as easy as setting the food data to 0 during startup and then manually adding food points and saturation whenever the food item is consumed. Rendering that data is my issue.

Specifically, I'm wanting to replace the values gotten by the FoodEventsEvent with ones from a JS array. This has worked to some extent, but I'm running into an issue where saturation values are about 3 times higher than expected. 

**Mostly, I'm curious as to whether I'm interacting with FoodValues correctly.** I surmise I'm not but I'm not sure how. Below is my script and some examples.

**If you have any insight to share on how FoodValues is intended to work (and why the rendered saturation is so much higher than what was passed to FoodValues), I would greatly appreciate hearing it.**

Thanks, MC7.

----------------------------------

Here is the script I'm using.
```
ForgeEvents.onEvent("squeek.appleskin.api.event.FoodValuesEvent", event => {
  try {
    let stack = event.itemStack
    let player = event.player

    let spoil_data = global.spoil_config[stack.id]
    let food_data = spoil_data[4]

    let spoil_max = spoil_data[2]

    let spoil_percentage = (stack.nbt?.spoilage / spoil_max)

    console.log("spoilage: " + stack.nbt?.spoilage + ", spoil max: " + spoil_max + "= spoil %: " + spoil_percentage)

    let hunger = 0
    let saturation = 0

    if (spoil_percentage < 0.25) {

        hunger = food_data[0] * 1.25
        saturation = food_data[1] * 1.25
    }   
    else if (spoil_percentage < 0.5) {

        hunger = food_data[0] * 0.9
        saturation = food_data[1] * 0.9
    }
    else if (spoil_percentage < 0.75) {

        hunger = food_data[0] * 0.75
        saturation = food_data[1] * 0.75

    }
    else if (spoil_percentage < 1.0) {

        hunger = food_data[0] * 0.4
        saturation = food_data[1] * 0.4

    }


    //hunger = Math.round(hunger) // commented out as it shouldn't have bearing on this issue, to my knowledge.
    //saturation = Math.round(hunger)

    if (stack.nbt) {
      let FoodValues = Java.loadClass("squeek.appleskin.api.food.FoodValues")
      //event.defaultFoodValues = new FoodValues(hunger, saturation)
      event.modifiedFoodValues = new FoodValues(hunger, saturation)
    }
  } catch (err) {
    console.error("[AppleSkin Override] Error handling food values for " + event.itemStack.id)
    console.error(err)
  }
})
```

As an aside, I have confirmed that each part of this script is properly pulling info from spoil_config.

Here are some results of this script. You'll notice that depending on the food value overridden, the results of this drastically change.

When using

> //event.defaultFoodValues
> event.modifiedFoodValues
>
<img width="357" height="135" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/44d17911-9d5e-4f9c-a38a-b63082e0c478" />

While the food point value of 3 is correct, the saturation value of 9 is not even close. spoil_config's saturation entry is 3.

When using
